00:13:56.020 --> 00:13:59.560
I can't believe how agile that thing is despite its huge body!

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:00.900
I have returned!

00:14:01.230 --> 00:14:04.320
Here's the data you requested, woof!

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:06.400
Well, aren't you a hard worker!

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:08.820
I'll meet a bitter end if you uninstall me, after all.

00:14:08.820 --> 00:14:09.820
You will?

00:14:09.820 --> 00:14:13.740
My individuality is linked to this PC!

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:15.790
Even though you're made up of only data?

00:14:15.790 --> 00:14:16.710
How would <i>you </i>like if—

00:14:16.710 --> 00:14:19.830
you transformed into a big bug 
when you wake up one morning?

00:14:19.830 --> 00:14:21.420
Like Gregor Samsa?

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:24.880
"You" stay the same, 
aside from your vessel that has changed.

00:14:24.880 --> 00:14:26.590
Oh. Th-This diagram...

00:14:26.590 --> 00:14:31.100
I couldn't find any matching 
data between figures 43 and 46.

00:14:31.100 --> 00:14:38.600
Hence I referenced your notes on this PC about higher-dimensional 
flying creatures to analogically infer my own research.

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:41.060
You read my notes!?

00:14:41.060 --> 00:14:43.730
I think it's a fascinating inquiry!

00:14:43.730 --> 00:14:45.400
Are you not going to publish it?

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:48.610
No way, the whole thing is 
hardly mapped out. It's a mess!

00:14:48.610 --> 00:14:50.610
Then leave the polishing and publishing to me!

00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:52.990
Sure, If you can. I guess.

00:14:53.530 --> 00:14:55.740
Roger, loud and clear!

00:14:59.080 --> 00:15:00.870
They said "3 billion."

00:15:01.540 --> 00:15:02.130
Pardon?

00:15:02.130 --> 00:15:08.470
That pterodactyl carcass—apparently a museum 
reached out with an asking price of 3 billion yen.

00:15:08.470 --> 00:15:13.140
If it's worth 3 billion, that skeleton we have buried 
underground must be worth its weight in gold.

00:15:14.050 --> 00:15:15.640
My, this is nostalgic.

00:15:15.640 --> 00:15:17.140
What even are these?

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:19.390
Microfiches. You've never seen them?

00:15:19.390 --> 00:15:22.150
A machine that can read these 
should be around here somewhere.

00:15:22.150 --> 00:15:23.980
I'll go find it.

00:15:25.270 --> 00:15:26.980
Wait, what does it even look like?

00:15:27.730 --> 00:15:30.490
That's an OHP. An overhead projector.

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:34.070
That's a 5-inch floppy drive and an 8-inch floppy drive.

00:15:34.070 --> 00:15:36.830
It looks more like a computer than those things!

00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:40.120
I found it! A microfiche reader.

00:15:40.910 --> 00:15:44.420
Seems like they have 
another 3 billion on their hands.

